Use a long-wavelength photon for the first observation. It gives only $x_1=x+O(\lambda),$ but its momentum is small enough that the particle receives only a gentle kick. Wait for a time $t$, then make a second long-wavelength observation: $x_2=x+vt+O(\lambda).$ The inferred displacement and velocity are $\begin{aligned}
 d&=x_2-x_1=vt+O(\lambda),\\
 v_{\mathrm{meas}}&=\frac{d}{t}
                   =v+O\!\left(\frac{\lambda}{t}\right).
 \end{aligned}$ No matter how large $\lambda$ is, the measurement contribution $\lambda/t$ can be made small by waiting long enough.

